### Registration

Competitors interested in participating in *pkmn.ai*'s ladders or tournaments can request
inclusion [via GitHub](https://github.com/pkmn/ai/issues/new?template=register.yml). You will be
required to provide:

  - your *project's name* (max 18 characters, following Pokémon Showdown username conventions;
    subject to staff discretion, prefer regex `^[A-Za-z ]+$`, distinct from other projects, must not
    include the tokens `poke` / `mon` / `pkmn` / `AI` / `bot` / `agent` / `showdown` / `smogon`,
    bare English Pokémon species names, or known players' names).
  - *GitHub and Smogon usernames* for each team member associated with your project (each person may
    belong to at most one project, and all team members must remain Smogon account holders in good
    standing to stay eligible).
  - *qualifying criteria* to justify participation in the hosted environment (_e.g._, [Pokémon
    Showdown](https://pokemonshowdown.com/ladder) / [vgc.bot](https://vgc.bot/leaderboard) /
    [pkmn.bot](https://pkmn.bot/leaderboard) rankings).
  - descriptive fields on *project details* for the [projects](/projects) page.
  - agreement to:

    1. adhere to the latest community guidance on [Responsible Releases](/release).
    2. grant *pkmn.ai* permission to run your model in its arena for the purpose of evaluation
       against other agents.
    3. grant *pkmn.ai* permission to run your model to aid in evaluating suspected cheating cases in
       Smogon & Pokémon Showdown competitions.

  - a PNG image (_optional_) with your *project's logo* to be displayed on your project's
    statistics page.

This will open up an issue on the [pkmn/ai](https://github.com/pkmn/ai) repository. Staff will
review this issue, fill in the appropriate information in
[`projects.yml`](https://github.com/pkmn/ai/blob/main/static/projects.yml), and approve your project
for submissions.

::: aside
The *pkmn.ai* arena competitions aim to provide the fairest way to evaluate the strength of
competitive Pokémon battling agents, and *projects are subject to removal at staff's discretion if
they are deemed to violate the spirit of this competition*. If you are looking to gain an edge or
game the system based on the specific nuances of the arena, instead of simply trying to produce a
strong agent that works in any environment, you have likely strayed in the wrong direction.
:::

### Submission

Registered competitors may access the [account](/account) page, which requires GitHub OAuth
credentials to authenticate. On this page you will be able to manage private account details
(_e.g._, associating a Discord user ID with your account to be used for notifications about your
submissions and for roles within the [chat](/chat) room) and view your account's status. Once your
account is approved and eligible for submissions, you can fill out a submission form with the
following information:

  - *`version`*: unique version string within your project (cannot be reused even after a failed
    smoke test; max 12 characters, _e.g._, `1.0.0` or `v2`, regex
    `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,11}$`).
  - *`artifact_url`*: direct HTTPS URL to a `.tar.gz`/`.tar.xz`/`.tar.zst` bundle _or_ an OCI
    container image reference (_e.g._, `ghcr.io/...`, `docker.io/...`).
  - *`sha256`*: 64-character hex SHA-256 checksum of the archive or OCI manifest digest.
  - *`auth_header`* (_optional_): read-only bearer token / header used once by the arena to pull
    private artifacts.
  - *`ladders`* / *`tournaments`*: non-empty subsets of the ladders or tournaments to enter your
    agent in.

Ladder submissions will run on an *Apple M6 Mac mini* with access to *10 cores* (8 Performance & 2
Efficiency), *14 GiB* of dedicated RAM, and 4 GiB of scratch `$TMPDIR` storage wiped between games.
The *network will be disabled* for all competitors. Competitors will be blind to the name of their
opponents. Ladder submissions may take the form of either an OCI `linux/arm64` container that can
access the *GPU* virtually via Vulkan passthrough, or a native bundle that has full native access
to the GPU/ANE via Metal/CoreML. Tournament hardware will be outlined in the tournament-specific
rules and will only support `linux/amd64` OCI containers.

{summary="*OCI Container*"}
::: details
```dockerfile
FROM --platform=linux/arm64 rust:1.85-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /src
COPY . .
RUN cargo build --release && strip --strip-unneeded target/release/my-agent

FROM --platform=linux/arm64 debian:bookworm-slim
COPY --from=builder /src/target/release/my-agent /usr/local/bin/my-agent
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/my-agent"]
```
:::

{summary="*Native Bundle*"}
::: details
```bash
mkdir -p dist/src dist/weights
uv python install 3.12
uv venv --python 3.12 --relocatable --no-cache dist/.venv
uv pip install --no-cache --python dist/.venv/bin/python mlx numpy
find dist/.venv -name '__pycache__' -type d -exec rm -rf {} +

cp src/*.py dist/src/
cp weights/*.safetensors dist/weights/

cat << 'EOF' > dist/run
#!/usr/bin/env bash
DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
exec "${DIR}/.venv/bin/python" -u "${DIR}/src/main.py" --weights "${DIR}/weights"
EOF
chmod +x dist/run
tar -czf agent-bundle.tar.gz -C dist .
```
:::

Every project submission must be at most *500 MiB* both compressed and unpacked on disk. Native
bundles must contain at most 25,000 files/directories/symlinks and an executable `./run` at the
archive root. OCI containers must contain at most 32 OCI layers and the container `ENTRYPOINT` /
`CMD` must run the agent.

Tournaments close at `00:00:00 UTC` strictly before start time and accept only the first successful
entry for a project (resubmissions are allowed after a failed smoke test until the cutoff). Ladder
competitors may have *up to 2 versions* of their project competing concurrently, but may only submit
a new version of their agent after both of the following conditions are true:

  - *14 days* have elapsed since their last successful ladder submission.
  - all of their *previous submissions have settled* to $`\text{SE}(R) \le 30\text{ Elo}`
    ($`\hat{\sigma} \le 0.173` nats, 95% CI $`\approx \pm 59\text{ Elo}`) and at least 25
    [blind-pair matches](#Matchmaking) (50 games).

A submission succeeds if the arena can unpack it and play through the first turn of a battle in the
format(s) it is entered in ("smoke test"). For ladder submissions, if there are already 2 versions
of a project on the same ladder, the weaker of the two versions will be automatically retired,
leaving the *strongest version of the agent and the latest submission*.

### Format

A *pkmn.ai* ladder is fixed to a specific time period and will not be updated if the community
later decides to [change](https://pkmn.cc/changes) the rules --- it is necessary to hold the format
constant to be able to compare to past versions. However, in order to support more dynamic
metagames, certain formats will operate on a seasonal model and will be retired and replaced with
more modern rules.

The competitive Pokémon formats currently eligible for *fixed* ladder submissions are:

- *RBY*: `gen1ou`
- *ADV*: `gen3ou`
- *SV*: `gen9ou`

These formats are defined to have the rules and mechanics from Pokémon Showdown as of Nov 9th 2026
([a78551b0](https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown/commit/a78551b0)/
[v0.11.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@pkmn/sim/v/0.11.0)). Strong human-made teams harvested from
Pokémon Showdown server logs will be provided to the competitors ([see below](#Protocol)), and
[data.pkmn.cc](https://data.pkmn.cc) formatted usage stats data computed from the period the teams
have been drawn from will be provided at `$PKMN_STATS_DIR/<format>.json`.

The competitive Pokémon formats currently eligible for *seasonal* ladder submissions are:

{summary="*RAND* (`gen9randombattle`)"}
::: details
This format is defined to have the rules and mechanics from Pokémon Showdown as of Nov 9th 2026
([a78551b0](https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown/commit/a78551b0)/
[v0.11.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@pkmn/sim/v/0.11.0)), though in Generation X this ladder
will be retired and replaced with one supporting `gen10randombattle` if Pokémon Showdown creates
one. Teams will be provided by the random team generator bundled with Pokémon Showdown, and
[data.pkmn.cc](https://data.pkmn.cc) formatted usage stats data computed from the period the teams
have been drawn from will be provided at `$PKMN_STATS_DIR` in the same hierarchy as exists on the
site.
:::

{summary="*VGC* (`gen9championsvgc2026regmc`)"}
::: details
This format is defined to have the rules and mechanics from Pokémon Showdown as of Nov 9th 2026
([a78551b0](https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown/commit/a78551b0)/
[v0.11.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@pkmn/sim/v/0.11.0)), played with *Closed Team Sheets*,
though it will be updated to follow TPCi's VGC ruleset --- once a new ruleset is announced, the
existing ladder will be retired and replaced with one for the new ruleset.

Unlike other *pkmn.ai* arena ladders, competitors in the *VGC* ladder will be required to submit
20 distinct teams to make up a shared competitor team pool and will be randomly assigned teams from
this shared pool at the start of each match. The arena ensures each team in the pool is played with
equal probability and that any match uses at most one team submitted by the two competing projects
combined (self-play across versions of the same project draws both teams exclusively from third
parties). Teams submitted to the pool must be
[distinct](https://github.com/pkmn/ai/blob/main/teams/README.md) from each other and from any
publicly shared teams. No usage statistics will be provided for the format.

To speak to the "spirit of this competition" referenced above: submitting degenerate teams to the
pool is considered to be contrary to the goals of the ladder (and pointless --- [blind-pair
matches](#Matchmaking) ensure this is most likely to simply result in ties), whereas submitting
teams you feel your agent has a better chance of mastering due to specific training or mastery over
niche mechanics is considered fair play.
:::

Ladders and tournaments hosted on *pkmn.ai* will all follow the exact same timer rules as Pokémon
Showdown (without Pokémon Showdown's 60-second disconnect grace); however, *canceling or undoing*
moves is disabled, as is *forfeiting or offering ties*. Furthermore,
[*pondering*](/glossary#pondering) will be disabled in ladder matches, though it will be enabled by
default in tournaments unless otherwise specified.

Logs or team information from the ladders using harvested human team data will never be made public.
*RAND* ladder games and tournament matches (logs and teams) are broadcast live --- with full
tournament replay archives (`.7z`) published upon completion --- and *VGC* ladder logs and teams
will be made available after the ladder is retired and rotated out for a new season.

### Protocol

All ladder and tournament matches on the *pkmn.ai* arena take place in a sandbox: your submission
is launched as a local subprocess for *one single game* (or one complete *Best-of-N series* in
multi-game tournaments, where initialization occurs once and scratch state persists across games of
the series) and communicates strictly over *standard input (`stdin`)* and *standard output
(`stdout`)* with no room-ID prefix lines.

  - there is no login protocol; an agent must write *`/ready`* to `stdout` --- or
    *`/utm <packed_team>`* with a legal team in bring-your-own-team (BYOT) tournaments --- once it
    has initialized and loaded any data or weights it needs to begin playing. Agents that fail to
    do so within *60 seconds* (or submit an illegal team in BYOT) will receive a game loss.
  - after `/ready` is received, in non-Random Battle formats with arena-assigned teams (ladders and
    pre-assigned tournaments), the very first line sent to your agent's `stdin` is:

    ```text
    |utm|<packed_team>
    ```

    This team data provides the full details of the team, unlike Pokémon Showdown's
    `|request|<JSON>` which only includes computed information. Note that Pokémon Showdown's [packed
    team format](https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown/blob/master/sim/TEAMS.md#packed-format)
    uses `|` as a separator, so naive splitting on `|` will fail.
  - agents read newline-delimited Pokémon Showdown protocol lines from `stdin` and *write a
    newline-terminated choice string to `stdout`* whenever they receive an active `|request|<JSON>`
    (where `request.wait` is falsy) (_e.g._, `move 1\n`, `move 2 terastallize\n`, `switch 3\n`,
    `team 123456\n`, or Doubles `move 1 +2, switch 3\n`). An authoritative clock line is sent on
    `stdin` immediately after every active `|request|<JSON>` to facilitate timer management:

    ```text
    |inactive|Time left: 150.0 sec this turn | 150.0 sec total | 0.0 sec grace
    ```

    Any other line written to `stdout` (including disabled commands) is ignored, but emitting more
    than 64 lines per decision or any line over 64 KiB forfeits the game as a crash.
  - on the ladder (but not tournaments): the arena coordinator utilizes *time-slicing* to share
    resources --- your entire process group will be paused (`SIGSTOP` or `podman pause`) the instant
    your choice newline arrives on `stdout`, and resumed (`SIGCONT` or `podman unpause`) only when
    it is your turn to act again, guaranteeing uninterrupted execution while unpaused.
  - if your agent makes an invalid or unavailable choice, its remaining turn budget continues with
    no turn increment credited and it receives the error, a `|request|<JSON>` (resent on invalid
    choices, updated by the simulator on unavailable choices), and an updated clock line:

    ```text
    |error|[Invalid choice] Can't move: ...
    |request|<JSON>
    |inactive|Time left: ...
    ```

    Emitting *3 consecutive `[Invalid choice]` errors* on the same request will result in a game
    loss; `[Unavailable choice]` errors (_e.g._, Encore, Disable, or trapping abilities) do not
    count toward this limit.
  - submissions that lose due to invalid choices / crashes / OOM / timeouts in 10 consecutive games
    (or over 20% of games after 10 games) will be *quarantined* across all ladders without
    resetting the 14-day cooldown.

### Matchmaking

Pokémon battles on the *pkmn.ai* arena ladders are always conducted in the form of *blind-pair
matches* --- a given pairing of agents will always play out two games against each other in
succession, with the teams swapped between games. Because all games on the ladder occur in a
sandbox, agents are unaware of any details from the first game as they play out the second, meaning
team-matchup variance can be eliminated.

Ratings are modeled using batch [Maximum a posteriori (MAP)
estimation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_a_posteriori_estimation) over a 5-category
[Davidson model (1970)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model#Ties) (a
generalization of the [Bradley–Terry
model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model) with ties) which considers match
outcomes as a unit. The ratings are mapped to the [Elo
scale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elo_rating_system), and one specific agent chosen to represent
*the top of the Pokémon Showdown human ladder is defined to be 1500 on the scale* (the "anchor").
This anchor will always be eligible for matchmaking and prevents the rating scale from drifting
over time. Leaderboards order entries by 95% conservative rating
($`\text{Elo} - 1.96 \times \text{SE}`), assigning an official rank to at most one active version
per project once past the provisional gate ($`\ge 10` matches and $`\text{SE} \le 60`).

The arena matchmaking algorithm aims to maximize [Shannon information
gain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_gain_(decision_tree)) over competitor ratings across
all ladders while prioritizing determining the strongest competitors and matching them against each
other, dampening repeat matchups, and preventing the outright starvation of any project or ladder.

Matchmaking and ratings in tournaments are dependent on the exact tournament rules, but by default
involve a Round Robin format where every competitor plays every other competitor for a fixed number
of games or matches, followed by a Superfinal where the top two entrants after the Round Robin
square off in an extended series to determine the winner.

### Recommendations

{summary="*Packaging*"}
::: details
  - strip debug symbols from compiled binaries and shared libraries (`strip -x` on macOS, `strip
    --strip-unneeded` on Linux).
  - quantize neural network weights (`float16`, `bfloat16`, or `int8` `.safetensors` / `.gguf`).
  - pre-compile CoreML `.mlpackage` models to `.mlmodelc` (`xcrun coremlcompiler compile
    Model.mlpackage ./weights/`) and exclude intermediate build files.
  - exclude `__pycache__`, `.git`, test suites, `pip`/`uv` caches, C++ header files (`include/`),
    and static archives (`.a`).
:::

{summary="*Uploading*"}
::: details
  - upload `.tar.gz` to a private Cloudflare R2 (10 GB/mo free tier, zero egress), AWS S3, Backblaze
    B2, or GCS bucket and generate a 24-hour presigned `GET` URL (`rclone link` or `aws s3 presign
    --expires-in 86400`).
  - host the `.tar.gz` on a private GitHub repository release and pass a fine-grained PAT scoped
    strictly to that single repo with `Contents: Read-only` (expiring in 7 days).
  - host the bundle in a private Hugging Face repo and provide a read-only token (`hf_...`) scoped
    to that repo.
  - push to a private registry and provide a temporary `read:packages` pull token.
:::

{summary="*Environment*"}
::: details
  - the wall-clock time jumps while you are paused --- if your agent records a timestamp on Turn N
    and compares it to a timestamp taken on Turn N+1, the difference will include all of your
    opponent's thinking time while your process was frozen. Instead, measure elapsed time from the
    moment your agent reads `|inactive|Time left: ...` on `stdin`.
  - finish state updates _before_ writing `\n` to `stdout` --- the arena coordinator calls
    `SIGSTOP` / `podman pause` within microseconds of reading your choice newline on `stdout`,
    meaning any post-turn tree pruning or cleanup will happen the next time you are unpaused if you
    do not handle it before making your choice. Conversely, always explicitly flush `stdout`
    immediately after making your choice in case your language runtime buffers `stdout` when
    connected to a pipe.
  - the bundle/container root filesystem is mounted read-only; `$TMPDIR`, `$HOME`, and (on ladders)
    `$XDG_CACHE_HOME` all point to the per-game scratch directory (wiped between games; native
    bundles cannot write to `/tmp`), which also holds CoreML/Metal shader caches and runtime temp
    files.
  - ladder processes and threads are capped at 256 (`RLIMIT_NPROC` / `--pids-limit=256`), and
    `stderr` is drained (with the last 4 KiB surfaced on [account](/account) if a ladder smoke test
    fails).
  - the arena sets `OMP_NUM_THREADS=10`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=10`, `TORCH_NUM_THREADS=10`, and
    `RAYON_NUM_THREADS=10`. Do not hardcode thread pools larger than 10 threads. Similarly,
    `ARENA_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB=14336` is set --- be sure to size runtime tables to account for potential
    runtime overhead and avoid triggering a forfeit due to running out of memory (OOM). In
    tournaments with pondering enabled, `ARENA_PONDER=1` is set.
  - on macOS, natively bundled submissions can take advantage of the fact that clean, read-only
    memory-mapped file pages (`mmap` with `PROT_READ` and `MAP_PRIVATE` or `MAP_SHARED`, such as
    `safetensors` or `gguf` memory mapping) are backed by the read-only bundle file on disk and
    count as 0 bytes toward the arena-enforced 14 GiB RAM limit.
:::
